_weight": 0.58, "fundamentals": {"margin": 13.2, "lean": 9.0, "environment": 4.3, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": -0.1}, "rating_margin": 16.0, "sigma_race": 4.5, "sigma_total": 4.94, "baseline_margin": 19.58, "analyst_adjustment": 1.42, "key_factors": ["California's 12-point Democratic lean and open-seat dynamics favor the Democrat", "Late polls consistently show Becerra ahead by 20-29 points", "All eight expert ratings call the race Solid or Safe D", "Prediction markets price Becerra at 97% to win"], "rationale": "Polling averages put Becerra up roughly 21-23 points, with every recent survey showing a double-digit Democratic lead, and markets and raters agree the race is effectively decided. The fundamentals baseline of about 13 points is the main reason to shade slightly below the polling average, but nothing suggests Hilton is closing the gap.", "watch": "A late turnout surge among wrong-direction voters, as some surveys show deep state pessimism, could narrow the margin more than expected.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Gavin Newsom (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "California's open governor's race pits Democrat Xavier Becerra against Republican Steve Hilton, with Gavin Newsom term-limited off the ballot. The state's 12-point Democratic lean anchors the race, and every major poll in late October shows Becerra leading by 20 points or more, including surveys from Berkeley IGS, PPIC, Emerson, and SurveyUSA. Even Republican-sponsored polls show Becerra well ahead.\n\nAll eight expert ratings call the race Solid or Safe Democratic, and prediction markets price Becerra's chances at 97 percent. Fundraising is roughly comparable, with Hilton slightly ahead in cash on hand, but money has not translated into movement in the polls. Headlines note record voter pessimism about the state's direction, yet that sentiment has not dented Becerra's wide lead as the election nears.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9963, "margin": 17.38}, {"dat